# Student LMS Coding Guidelines

## Core Standards
- Use TypeScript strict mode patterns; avoid `any` unless absolutely necessary.
- Write type-safe code in both frontend and backend; prefer explicit domain types, validated inputs, and safe narrowing over type assertions.
- Reuse existing components, utilities.
- Prefer `shadcn/ui` and Radix UI primitives wherever possible before building custom UI from scratch.
- Prefer icons from a maintained icon library (for example, `@phosphor-icons/react`) over image URLs or custom icon assets wherever possible.
- Keep components focused and readable; extract reusable logic when duplication appears.
- Keep file size minimal; max file length is 200 lines. Split into smaller components/hooks/utils when approaching this limit.
- Handle edge cases explicitly (empty states, invalid inputs, null/undefined values).

## API Architecture
- Expose all backend functionality as REST API routes only (file routes under
  `src/routes/api/**` with `server.handlers` for GET/POST/etc.). Do not add new
  TanStack `createServerFn` server functions for new work.
- Structure each endpoint as route handler → handler (`src/server/api/**/handlers`)
  → service (`src/server/api/**/*.service.ts`), and call it from the client via
  the typed `fetchJson` helpers in `src/lib/api/**`.
- Access the database through the Drizzle query builder (`db.select`, `db.insert`,
  `db.update`, `db.delete` from `@/db`). Avoid raw `sql` template queries unless a
  query genuinely cannot be expressed with the builder.

## Testing Requirements
- Add or update tests for every non-trivial behavior change.
- Prefer behavior-driven tests with Testing Library over implementation details.
- Cover positive path, fallback/edge case, and critical UI state transitions.
- Keep tests deterministic; avoid timing-sensitive assertions where possible.
- Write tests side by side with implementation in the same PR; do not defer tests to follow-up work.
- Use `vitest` for unit tests and keep test files colocated or clearly paired with source files (for example, `foo.ts` + `foo.test.ts`).
- Coverage target for this repo is 100%; at minimum, any touched module must stay at 100% statement/branch/function/line coverage.

## Testing Documentation
- Keep `docs/testing/feature-test-matrix.md` updated for every touched feature.
- Add or update `docs/testing/features/<feature>.md` when tests are added/changed/removed.
- Complete `docs/testing/pr-checklist.md` in every PR that changes feature behavior or test setup.
- Ensure documented test commands are valid and runnable.

## Styling and UX
- Use the shared Masai design system for UI primitives instead of one-off styles:
  - **Buttons and CTAs**: `MasaiButton` (`src/components/ui/masai-button.tsx`) for primary actions; `CardCtaButton` (`src/components/shared/card-cta-button.tsx`) for card-level CTAs. Do not build custom button/CTA markup with ad-hoc colors or padding.
  - **Other controls**: Prefer existing `masai-*` components in `src/components/ui/` (inputs, chips, tabs, drawers, pagination, etc.) before creating new variants.
  - **Colors**: Use Masai color tokens from `src/lib/masai-colors.css` (e.g. `primary-*`, `gray-*`) via Tailwind classes. Avoid hardcoded hex/rgb values when a token exists.
  - **Typography**: Use Masai typography classes from `src/lib/masai-typography.css` (e.g. `type-h*`, `type-b*`, `type-s*`). Do not invent custom font sizes/weights for standard UI text.
- Follow existing Tailwind and component style conventions in the same feature area.
- Avoid hardcoded magic values when a project token or shared constant exists.
- Preserve accessibility basics (semantic elements, interactive affordances, readable text).

## Backend and API Structure
- **Prefer REST APIs** for new and migrated features. Expose HTTP endpoints under `src/routes/api/**` using TanStack Start `server.handlers` (GET/POST/etc.), not `createServerFn`, unless there is a documented exception.
- Keep route files thin: parse request → call a handler in `src/server/api/<feature>/handlers/` → return `Response.json`.
- Put shared HTTP concerns in `src/server/api/http/` (auth, JSON responses, error mapping) and request parsing in `src/server/api/<feature>/utils/`.
- Put domain/business logic in `src/server/<feature>/services/*` and presentation helpers in `src/server/<feature>/utils/*` (for example `learn/services/*`). Route handlers must not contain SQL or heavy business rules.
- Call REST endpoints through typed helpers in `src/lib/api/*` (for example `learn/learnApi.ts`) using `fetch` with `credentials: 'same-origin'`. Use the same helpers in route loaders and React Query so SSR and CSR share one HTTP contract.
- Do not write raw SQL queries in application code. Prefer typed ORM query builders (Drizzle) for safety, consistency, and maintainability.
- Document REST contracts under `docs/api-responses/<feature>/` (method, path, query/body, success and error shapes).

## PR Quality Bar
- Ensure `npm run test`, `npm run lint`, and `npm run typecheck` pass for touched code.
- Ensure TypeScript has zero errors for changed code paths; target 0 TypeScript errors in the project.
- Keep changes scoped to the request; do not refactor unrelated areas.
- Add concise code comments only where logic is non-obvious.
